<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.18. The final Fourier transform applied to Laplace's Equation</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>
Recall
<definition xml:id="def-fourier-transform-">
  <statement>
    <p>
      The <term>Fourier Transform</term> of a function <m>f(x)</m> denoted <m>\mathcal{F}[f](\omega)</m> or simply <m>\mathcal{F}[f]</m> is
      <md>
        \mathcal{F}[f](\omega) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)e^{i\omega x}~dx.
      </md>
      Note we input a function of <m>x</m> and output a function of <m>\omega</m>. The 
      <term>Inverse Fourier Transform</term> <m>\mathcal{F}^{-1}</m> is
      <md>
        f(x) = \int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} \mathcal{F}(\omega)e^{-i\omega x}~d\omega.
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<fact xml:id="fact-fourier-transform-properties">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Properties of the Fourier Transform:</term>
      <ol>
        <li> Spatial derivatives become multiplication:
          <md>
            \mathcal{F}[f'] = -i\omega \mathcal{F}[f]
          </md>
        </li>
        <li>Fourier transform applied to Gaussian Distributions:
          <md>
            \mathcal{F}[\frac{\pi}{m}e^{\frac{-x^2}{4m}}] = e^{-m\omega^2}
          </md>
        </li>
        <li>Fourier transform of convolution of functions:
          <md>
          \mathcal{F}[f]\mathcal{F}[g] = \frac1{2\pi} \mathcal{F}[f * g]
          </md>
        </li>
        <li>Temporal derivative independent of Fourier transform:
          <md>
            \mathcal{F}[\pd{u}{t}] = \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \mathcal{F}[u]
          </md>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</fact>

<remark>
  <p>
    To solve the heat or wave equation using Fourier Transforms:
    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Step 1</title>
        <p>
          Take the Fourier Transform of both sides.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Step 2</title>
        <p>
          Solve in frequency world: <m>\mathcal{F}[u(x,t)] = \mathcal{F}[\ldots]</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Step 3</title>
        <p>
          Apply the Inverse Fourier Transform to both sides to get <m>u(x,t)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </p>
</remark>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>\alpha&gt;0</m> is a constant, explain why
  <md>
  \fourier\left[\frac{2\alpha}{x^2+\alpha^2}\right] =
  e^{-|\omega|\alpha}.
  </md></p>
      <p>Suggestion:  start with
  <md>
  \fourier^{-1}\left[e^{-|\omega|\alpha}\right] =
  \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-|\omega|\alpha} e^{-i\omega x}~d\omega = 
  \int_{-\infty}^0 e^{\omega\alpha} e^{-i\omega x}~d\omega +
  \int_{0}^\infty e^{-\omega\alpha} e^{-i\omega x}~d\omega.
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Solve Laplace's equation <m>\nabla^2 u = 0</m> for the function
  <m>u(x,y)</m> on the upper-half plane (where <m>{y\geq 0}</m>) with the boundary
  conditions
  <md>
  \begin{aligned}
    \lim_{y\to\infty} u(x,y) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x,0) \amp  = f(x)
  \end{aligned}
  </md>
  Start by defining the Fourier transform
  <md>
  \mathcal{F}[u(x,y)] = \cu(\omega, y) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty
  u(x,y)e^{i\omega x}~dx
  </md>
  and explain why
  <md>
  \fourier[u(x,y)] = \fourier[f]e^{-|\omega| y}.
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
